---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const APP_URL = "https://trading.onquantex.com";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Learn"
  description="A plain-English guide to spot trading and how Quantex's Grid, DCA, and Momentum bots actually work under the hood."
>
  <section class="qx-learn-hero">
    <div class="qx-container">
      <span class="qx-eyebrow">Learn</span>
      <h1>How trading, and the bots, actually work</h1>
      <p class="qx-lead">
        Not a sales pitch, just the mechanics: what happens when you place
        an order yourself, and what each bot is actually doing with your
        money while you're not watching.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="qx-section">
    <div class="qx-container qx-prose">
      <h2>Spot trading, in plain English</h2>
      <p>
        "Spot" just means you're buying or selling a coin at whatever
        price the market is at right now, and you own the coin outright
        the moment the trade fills, no leverage, no expiry date.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every price you see is really two prices: the <strong>bid</strong>
        (the highest price someone's currently offering to buy at) and the
        <strong>ask</strong> (the lowest price someone's offering to sell
        at). The gap between them is the spread. Place a
        <strong>market order</strong> and you take whatever the best
        available bid/ask is, right now, guaranteed to fill. Place a
        <strong>limit order</strong> instead and you name your own price
        and wait, it only fills if the market comes to you, but it might
        never fill at all.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="qx-section qx-section-alt">
    <div class="qx-container qx-prose">
      <h2>What a trading bot actually does</h2>
      <p>
        A bot isn't intelligence and it isn't magic. It's a small program
        running on a schedule, checking a condition, and placing the same
        kind of buy/sell order you could place by hand, just without
        needing you to sit and watch a chart at 3am. Every strategy below
        boils down to "check something, then act on a rule", the
        difference between them is what they check and when they act.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of them predict the future or guarantee a profit. A bot
        working a bad market condition for its strategy will lose money
        just as a human would, it just does it on a schedule instead of on
        a hunch.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="qx-section">
    <div class="qx-container qx-prose">
      <h2>Grid bots</h2>
      <p>
        You set a price range and a number of steps ("grid levels") inside
        it. The bot lays a ladder of buy orders below the current price
        and sell orders above it, spaced evenly through that range. As the
        price ticks up and down inside the range, it keeps buying the dips
        and selling the bounces, over and over, off the same back-and-forth
        motion.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Works best when:</strong> the price is oscillating sideways
        inside a range rather than committing to a direction.
        <strong>Struggles when:</strong> the price breaks hard through one
        edge of the range and keeps going, the bot is left holding a
        position on the wrong side of a move it wasn't built to follow.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="qx-section qx-section-alt">
    <div class="qx-container qx-prose">
      <h2>DCA bots</h2>
      <p>
        Short for dollar-cost averaging. The bot buys a fixed amount on a
        fixed schedule, hourly, daily, whatever you set, no matter what
        the price happens to be doing that moment. Over enough buys, your
        average entry price smooths out toward the market's average over
        that window instead of depending on the one moment you happened to
        click "buy".
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Works best when:</strong> you'd rather not try to time an
        entry at all, and are building a position over weeks or months.
        <strong>Struggles when:</strong> compared to a single lump-sum buy
        right before a sustained rally, it will lag behind, averaging in
        gives up some upside in exchange for not needing to guess the
        bottom.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="qx-section">
    <div class="qx-container qx-prose">
      <h2>Momentum bots</h2>
      <p>
        This one tries to catch a trend once it's actually confirmed,
        rather than guessing one is coming. It watches for a signal, a
        moving-average crossover (a short-term average price crossing
        above or below a longer-term one) or an RSI reading moving out of
        overbought/oversold territory, and enters once that signal fires,
        then exits again once the signal reverses or the move stalls.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Works best when:</strong> the market is actually trending,
        clean directional moves with real follow-through.
        <strong>Struggles when:</strong> the market is choppy and
        indecisive, signals can fire and reverse quickly (a "whipsaw"),
        catching the bot in and back out for a small loss each time.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="qx-section qx-section-alt">
    <div class="qx-container qx-prose">
      <h2>So which one do you actually want?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Market's stuck in a range, not clearly trending either way? Grid tends to fit that best.</li>
        <li>Don't want to think about timing at all, just building a position steadily? DCA.</li>
        <li>A real trend is forming and you want to ride it? Momentum.</li>
        <li>Want to make the call yourself, order by order? That's what spot trading is for, no bot required.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Quantex's <a href="/ai-hub">AI Hub</a> reads current price action
        and volatility for you and can suggest a reasonable starting point
        for any of these, but the choice, and the risk, is always yours.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="qx-cta">
    <div class="qx-container qx-cta-inner">
      <div>
        <h2>Ready to put this into practice?</h2>
        <p>Create an account, deposit crypto, and try a strategy for yourself.</p>
        <a class="qx-btn-primary qx-btn-lg" href={`${APP_URL}/signup`}>Trade Now</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .qx-learn-hero {
    padding: var(--space-48) 0 var(--space-24);
  }
  .qx-eyebrow {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-data);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--teal-base);
    background: var(--teal-pale);
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-8);
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-16);
  }
  .qx-learn-hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(32px, 4.5vw, 48px);
    color: var(--ink-base);
    max-width: 20ch;
  }
  .qx-lead {
    margin-top: var(--space-16);
    font-size: 18px;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .qx-section {
    padding: var(--space-48) 0;
  }
  .qx-section-alt {
    background: var(--cream-deep);
  }
  /* .qx-prose itself (max-width, h2/p/ul sizing) lives in global.css. */

  .qx-cta {
    padding: var(--space-48) 0 var(--space-32);
  }
  .qx-cta-inner {
    background: var(--teal-deep);
    border-radius: var(--radius-2xl);
    padding: var(--space-48);
    text-align: center;
  }
  .qx-cta-inner h2 {
    color: var(--on-accent);
  }
  .qx-cta-inner p {
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
    color: var(--teal-sage);
    max-width: 48ch;
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
  }
  .qx-cta-inner .qx-btn-primary {
    margin-top: var(--space-16);
  }

  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .qx-container {
      padding: 0 var(--space-16);
    }
    .qx-learn-hero {
      padding: var(--space-32) 0 var(--space-16);
    }
    .qx-learn-hero h1 {
      font-size: 26px;
    }
    .qx-lead {
      font-size: 16px;
    }
    .qx-section {
      padding: var(--space-32) 0;
    }
    .qx-cta-inner {
      padding: var(--space-32) var(--space-24);
    }
  }
</style>
